# Testing rules

There are two separate questions to ask of a rule, and a different way to answer
each:

- **Does the condition fire when I want it to, and stay quiet when I do not?**
  Replay your own past readings through it.
- **Does the alert actually reach me?** Send a real test alert with **Fire
  saved rule** (see [Building a rule](/docs/alerts/building-rules#testing-a-rule)).

This page is about the first.

## Replay in the app

Replay runs your rules against readings you have already had and shows when
each would have fired. Nothing is sent to anyone.

There are two ways in:

- From a rule's editor, press **Replay against history**. This replays the rule
  as it is in the editor, including changes you have not saved yet, alongside
  your other rules. Pick a time from **Historic firings** to open the day it
  really fired.
- From **Alerts > Simulator**, which replays all your switched-on rules over a
  day you choose.

<Screenshot id="alerts-simulator" />

The graph shows your readings over the window, with a play control underneath
that steps through it and a tick for every alert. Below that is the list of
alerts the replay found, with the time each would have fired. Beside the graph
are your rules, each with a switch to leave it out of the replay; open one to
see when each of its conditions was true.

### What Replay can and cannot tell you

Replay checks your rules every 5 minutes through the day, using the readings
and other data Nocturne had at each moment. That makes it good at answering
"would this rule have fired?", with some limits:

- **It cannot replay what people did.** Snoozes and acknowledgements are not
  replayed, so a replay shows every time a rule would have fired, not what
  would have been sent after someone snoozed it.
- **Do Not Disturb:** times you switched Do Not Disturb on by hand are replayed,
  and fires during them are marked as silenced. Your recurring quiet-hours
  schedule is not, because Nocturne does not keep a history of changes to it.
- **Gaps in readings** are filled with the last reading, checked every 5
  minutes. Around a gap, a rule with a "for at least" setting can fire sooner in
  a replay than it did at the time. See
  [When readings stop](/docs/alerts/sustained#when-readings-stop).
- A single replay covers at most two days.

## Playground

Nocturne's own alert engine and Replay, running in your browser on made-up
days. Pick a day, edit the rules or add example ones, and press play. The
lights beside each condition show which were true at the playhead. Carbs and
insulin on board appear on the graph when a rule uses them.

<AlertSimulator
  scenario="overnight-low"
  scenarios={["overnight-low", "brief-dip", "fast-fall", "sensor-gap", "meal-high", "missed-bolus", "treated-low"]}
  rules={["sustained-low", "low-untreated"]}
  editable
/>

Some things to try:

- On **Brief dip**, change "Low for 15 minutes" to 5 minutes.
- On **Treated low**, compare "Low, unless carbs are on board" with plain "Low".
- On **Missed bolus**, add "High with no insulin on board".
- On **Sensor gap**, add "Signal loss".

A change a real save would refuse, such as "for at least" 0 minutes, is flagged
here too, because the playground uses the same check as the rule editor. The
playground offers the conditions that need only glucose, carbs, insulin and the
clock; the app has many more (see [Conditions](/docs/alerts/conditions)).

<Callout type="info" title="Made-up readings, not advice">

  The example days are invented to show one behaviour each, and the rules are
  illustrations, not settings to copy. Test your real rules with Replay on your
  own readings, and agree your thresholds with your care team.

</Callout>
